"\"## Python decorators in plain English  \\n\\nA **decorator** is just a *function that wraps another function (or method) and adds something extra to it*.  \\nThink of it like putting a piece of tape around a gift: the tape doesn\\u2019t change the gift itself, but it lets you do something extra (e.g., label it, protect it, make it look nicer) without opening the gift.\\n\\nIn code, the \\u201cgift\\u201d is a regular function, and the \\u201ctape\\u201d is the decorator.  \\nWhen you call the decorated function, Python first runs the decorator\\u2019s wrapper, which can:\\n\\n* run code **before** the original function,\\n* run code **after** the original function,\\n* modify the arguments or the return value,\\n* even decide **whether** to call the original function at all.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. The building blocks\\n\\n### 1.1 A normal function\\n\\n```python\\ndef greet(name):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Say hello.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    return f\\\"Hello, {name}!\\\"\\n```\\n\\nCalling it:\\n\\n```python\\n>>> greet(\\\"Alice\\\")\\n'Hello, Alice!'\\n```\\n\\n### 1.2 A wrapper function\\n\\nNow we write a *wrapper* that adds a little print\\u2011statement before and after the original function runs.\\n\\n```python\\ndef my_wrapper(func):\\n    # `func` is the function we are going to wrap\\n    def inner(*args, **kwargs):\\n        print(\\\">>> About to call the function\\\")\\n        result = func(*args, **kwargs)   # run the original function\\n        print(\\\">>> Function finished\\\")\\n        return result                     # give back whatever the original returned\\n    return inner          # we return the new function (the wrapper)\\n```\\n\\n### 1.3 Using the wrapper manually\\n\\n```python\\n>>> wrapped_greet = my_wrapper(greet)   # apply the wrapper\\n>>> wrapped_greet(\\\"Bob\\\")\\n>>> About to call the function\\n>>> Function finished\\n'Hello, Bob!'\\n```\\n\\nWe just *replaced* `greet` with a new function (`inner`) that does extra work.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. The \\u201c@\\u201d syntax \\u2013 the decorator shortcut\\n\\nWriting `wrapped = my_wrapper(greet)` works, but Python gives us a nicer way:\\n\\n```python\\n@my_wrapper          # <- this line *applies* the decorator\\ndef greet(name):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Say hello.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    return f\\\"Hello, {name}!\\\"\\n```\\n\\nThe line `@my_wrapper` tells Python:  \\n\\n> \\u201cTake the function that follows (`greet`) and pass it to `my_wrapper`.  \\n>  Then bind the result back to the name `greet`.\\u201d\\n\\nNow `greet` is already wrapped:\\n\\n```python\\n>>> greet(\\\"Carol\\\")\\n>>> About to call the function\\n>>> Function finished\\n'Hello, Carol!'\\n```\\n\\nThat\\u2019s all a decorator is\\u2014just a function that receives another function and returns a new one.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. A real\\u2011world example: timing how long a function takes\\n\\nSuppose you want to know how long a function runs. You could sprinkle `time.time()` calls everywhere, but a decorator lets you do it once and reuse it.\\n\\n```python\\nimport time\\nfrom functools import wraps   # helps preserve the original function's metadata\\n\\ndef timer(func):\\n    @wraps(func)               # keeps __name__, __doc__, etc. unchanged\\n    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):\\n        start = time.time()\\n        result = func(*args, **kwargs)   # call the original\\n        end = time.time()\\n        print(f\\\"{func.__name__} took {end - start:.4f}s\\\")\\n        return result\\n    return wrapper\\n```\\n\\nUse it:\\n\\n```python\\n@timer\\ndef slow_add(a, b):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Add two numbers after a short pause.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    time.sleep(0.7)\\n    return a + b\\n\\n>>> slow_add(3, 4)\\nslow_add took 0.7012s\\n7\\n```\\n\\nAll the timing logic lives in `timer`; the actual function (`slow_add`) stays clean and focused on its job.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Decorators with *arguments* (a decorator that takes parameters)\\n\\nSometimes you want the decorator itself to be configurable:\\n\\n```python\\ndef repeat(times):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Run the decorated function `times` times.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    def decorator(func):\\n        @wraps(func)\\n        def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):\\n            for i in range(times):\\n                print(f\\\"--- Run {i+1}/{times} ---\\\")\\n                result = func(*args, **kwargs)\\n            return result          # return the last call's result\\n        return wrapper\\n    return decorator\\n```\\n\\nNotice the **three** layers:\\n\\n1. `repeat(times)` \\u2013 called once when Python reads the `@repeat(3)` line.\\n2. `decorator(func)` \\u2013 receives the function to be wrapped.\\n3. `wrapper(*args, **kwargs)` \\u2013 the actual replacement that runs each time the function is called.\\n\\nUse it:\\n\\n```python\\n@repeat(3)\\ndef say_hi():\\n    print(\\\"Hi!\\\")\\n\\n>>> say_hi()\\n--- Run 1/3 ---\\nHi!\\n--- Run 2/3 ---\\nHi!\\n--- Run 3/3 ---\\nHi!\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Built\\u2011in decorators you\\u2019ll see a lot\\n\\n| Decorator | What it does | Typical use |\\n|-----------|--------------|--------------|\\n| `@staticmethod` | Turns a method into a plain function that lives inside a class (no `self` argument). | Utility functions that don\\u2019t need the instance. |\\n| `@classmethod` | Passes the class itself (`cls`) instead of an instance (`self`). | Alternate constructors, factory methods. |\\n| `@property` | Lets you access a method like an attribute (`obj.x` instead of `obj.x()`). | Computed read\\u2011only attributes. |\\n| `@functools.lru_cache` | Caches the results of a function (memoization). | Speed up expensive pure functions. |\\n| `@dataclasses.dataclass` | Generates `__init__`, `__repr__`, `__eq__`, etc., for a class automatically. | Simple data containers. |\\n\\nThese are all just functions that follow the same \\u201ctake a callable, return a callable\\u201d pattern.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. A quick checklist for beginners\\n\\n| \\u2705 What to remember | \\ud83d\\udcda Why it matters |\\n|----------------------|-------------------|\\n| A decorator **receives a function** (or method) and **returns a new function**. | It\\u2019s the \\u201cwrapper\\u201d that adds extra behavior. |\\n| The `@decorator` line is syntactic sugar for `func = decorator(func)`. | Makes code tidy and readable. |\\n| Use `*args, **kwargs` in the wrapper to accept *any* signature. | So the decorator works with any function. |\\n| Use `functools.wraps` (or `@wraps`) inside your wrapper. | Preserves the original function\\u2019s name, docstring, and other metadata\\u2014helps debugging and tools like `help()`. |\\n| Decorators can be **nested** \\u2013 the one closest to the function runs **first**. | Allows you to stack behaviors (e.g., `@login_required @timer`). |\\n| A \\u201cdecorator with arguments\\u201d is actually **a function that returns a decorator**. | Gives you flexibility (e.g., `@repeat(5)`). |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Mini\\u2011exercise \\u2013 write your own \\u201cdebug\\u201d decorator\\n\\n**Goal:** Print the function name, the arguments it received, and the value it returns.\\n\\n```python\\nfrom functools import wraps\\n\\ndef debug(func):\\n    @wraps(func)\\n    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):\\n        args_repr   = \\\", \\\".join(repr(a) for a in args)\\n        kwargs_repr = \\\", \\\".join(f\\\"{k}={v!r}\\\" for k, v in kwargs.items())\\n        signature   = \\\", \\\".join(filter(None, [args_repr, kwargs_repr]))\\n        print(f\\\"Calling {func.__name__}({signature})\\\")\\n        result = func(*args, **kwargs)\\n        print(f\\\"{func.__name__} returned {result!r}\\\")\\n        return result\\n    return wrapper\\n```\\n\\nTest it:\\n\\n```python\\n@debug\\ndef add(a, b):\\n    return a + b\\n\\n@debug\\ndef greet(name=\\\"World\\\"):\\n    print(f\\\"Hello, {name}!\\\")\\n\\nadd(2, 5)\\ngreet()\\ngreet(name=\\\"Ada\\\")\\n```\\n\\nYou should see something like:\\n\\n```\\nCalling add(2, 5)\\nadd returned 7\\nCalling greet()\\nHello, World!\\ngreet returned None\\nCalling greet(name='Ada')\\nHello, Ada!\\ngreet returned None\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. TL;DR (the one\\u2011sentence summary)\\n\\n> A decorator is a callable that takes another function (or method) and returns a new function that adds extra behavior\\u2014usually written with the `@decorator_name` syntax for readability.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Want to go deeper?\\n\\n* Read the official docs: <https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/controlflow.html#decorators>\\n* Play with `functools.wraps`, `functools.lru_cache`, and `inspect.signature`.\\n* Try building a **permission** decorator for a small Flask app, or a **retry** decorator that re\\u2011runs a flaky network call.\\n\\nHappy decorating! \\ud83d\\ude80\""